module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ToLin
public import Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Mul

/-!
# Consistency of generator and parity-check matrices: `H * G = 0`

Following Nielsen & Chuang, *Quantum Computation and Quantum Information*, §10.4.1, a linear code
has two equivalent presentations:

* a **generator matrix** `G` (an `n × k` matrix), whose code is the span of its columns — the range
  of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` (`Matrix.range_mulVecLin`);
* a **parity-check matrix** `H` (an `(n − k) × n` matrix), whose code is its kernel — the set of
  words `x` with `H *ᵥ x = 0` (`LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin`; N&C eq. (10.57)).

When `H` and `G` describe *the same* code, every column of `G` is a codeword and hence annihilated
by `H`; equivalently the two matrices satisfy `H * G = 0`.

This file proves **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.18** (p. 447): *the parity-check matrix `H` and
generator matrix `G` for the same linear code satisfy `H G = 0`.*

## Main results

* `Matrix.mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_le_ker`: the underlying linear-algebra fact — if the code
  generated by `G` is contained in the code checked by `H`
  (`range G.mulVecLin ≤ ker H.mulVecLin`), then `H * G = 0`. Only containment is needed: the columns
  of `G` are among the words `H` annihilates.
* `Matrix.mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_eq_ker`: **Exercise 10.18** — when `H` and `G` present the
  *same* code (`range G.mulVecLin = ker H.mulVecLin`), `H * G = 0`.

## Implementation notes

`H * G = 0` is checked column by column (`Matrix.ext_of_mulVec_single`): column `j` of `H * G` is
`H *ᵥ (G *ᵥ eⱼ)` (`Matrix.mulVec_mulVec`), and `G *ᵥ eⱼ = G.mulVecLin eⱼ` is a codeword generated by
`G`, so it lies in `ker H.mulVecLin` by hypothesis and `H` sends it to `0`. The essential ingredient
is therefore just `range G.mulVecLin ≤ ker H.mulVecLin`, which the `_le` lemma isolates — the
exercise's "same code" is the special case of equality.

The coefficients range over an arbitrary commutative semiring `R` (no subtraction is used), so the
result covers N&C's binary codes (`R = ZMod 2`) and, as N&C note in Exercise 10.19, codes over more
general fields.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Matrix

variable {R : Type*} [CommSemiring R] {l m n : Type*} [Fintype m] [Fintype n]

/-- If the code generated by `G` — the span of its columns, `LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin` — is
contained in the code checked by `H` — the kernel `LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin` — then `H * G = 0`.
Every column of `G` is then a word annihilated by `H`, which is exactly `H * G = 0` read column by
column. This is the linear-algebra content behind Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.18: only containment
`range G ≤ ker H`, not equality of the two codes, is needed. -/
theorem mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_le_ker (H : Matrix l m R) (G : Matrix m n R)
    (h : LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin ≤ LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin) :
    H * G = 0 := by
  classical
  refine Matrix.ext_of_mulVec_single fun j => ?_
  rw [Matrix.zero_mulVec, ← Matrix.mulVec_mulVec]
  simpa only [LinearMap.mem_ker, Matrix.mulVecLin_apply] using
    h (LinearMap.mem_range_self G.mulVecLin (Pi.single j 1))

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.18** (p. 447): the parity-check matrix `H` and generator matrix
`G` for the same linear code satisfy `H * G = 0`. The two matrices present the same code exactly
when the code generated by `G` — the span of its columns, `LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin` — equals the
code checked by `H` — the kernel `LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin` (N&C eq. (10.57)); the columns of `G`
are then codewords, each annihilated by `H`. -/
theorem mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_eq_ker (H : Matrix l m R) (G : Matrix m n R)
    (h : LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin = LinearMap.ker H.mulVecLin) :
    H * G = 0 :=
  mul_eq_zero_of_range_mulVecLin_le_ker H G h.le

end Matrix
